From 46919a15da274ffa74a9fbb55ca0d9178c05d68c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 10:33:10 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] prplmesh: Improve On-Boot-Scan reliability and result-status
 accuracy

ChannelScanTask's On-Boot-Scan is triggered exactly once per Agent boot
and has no other opportunity to produce results for the remainder of the
session if that single trigger fails or if its results are later lost.
This change addresses several related issues that could cause On-Boot-Scan
to either fail outright or report inaccurate results to the controller.

1. Preserve stored channel-scan results across BACKHAUL_MANAGER_ENABLE
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   handle_backhaul_enable_event() unconditionally re-initialized every
   channel's entry in radio->channel_scan_results to SCAN_NOT_COMPLETED
   whenever the BACKHAUL_MANAGER_ENABLE event fired. This event is not a
   one-time occurrence - it can legitimately be received multiple times
   within a single agent session (e.g. on every backhaul-manager FSM
   re-exchange), and is not always preceded by an explicit results reset.

   If a successful On-Boot-Scan (or any other scan) had already stored
   real results before a later, redundant BACKHAUL_MANAGER_ENABLE event
   fired, those valid results were silently overwritten back to
   SCAN_NOT_COMPLETED. A subsequent "return stored results of last
   successful scan" request would then incorrectly report every channel
   as not completed, even though a successful scan had already run.

   Fix: only initialize a channel's entry to SCAN_NOT_COMPLETED if no
   result is already stored for that channel, instead of unconditionally
   overwriting it. This preserves any valid results already collected
   across redundant BACKHAUL_MANAGER_ENABLE events, while the explicit
   certification reset path (which clears channel_scan_results before
   this event fires) remains unaffected.

2. Cross-check On-Boot-Scan "stored results" against the real AgentDB status
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   handle_on_boot_scan_request()'s internal helper for answering a
   "return stored results of last successful scan" request built its
   channel/status list purely from an in-memory record of which channels
   were *attempted*, which is populated optimistically at trigger time -
   before the scan has actually run or completed. If the triggered scan
   subsequently failed, that failure was never propagated back into this
   record, only into a local, transient object that gets destroyed
   afterwards. As a result, a later "stored results" request could report
   "Success" with zero neighbors for a channel whose scan had actually
   failed.

   Fix: cross-check each channel against the real persisted status in
   radio->channel_scan_results before reporting it as SUCCESS - mirroring
   how the equivalent logic in the generic/controller-initiated scan path
   already does this correctly. If the AgentDB has no entry, or a
   non-SUCCESS status, for a given channel, the report now reflects the
   real outcome instead of the optimistic "attempted" status.

3. Delay the On-Boot-Scan trigger to avoid colliding with other startup work
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   The On-Boot-Scan trigger is sent from the same state/event that also
   kicks off a dense, one-time burst of unrelated startup work (various
   service initializations, interface re-enumeration, and a topology
   exchange). This burst can starve the event loop enough that the
   one-shot, non-retried scan-trigger request fails outright, permanently
   losing the single opportunity On-Boot-Scan gets to populate real
   results for that boot cycle.

   Fix: defer the On-Boot-Scan trigger CMDU by a short, configurable delay
   (via a one-shot timer) instead of sending it immediately, giving the
   startup burst time to settle first. If timer creation fails, the
   trigger falls back to firing immediately (previous behavior).

4. Add a bounded, automatic retry for a failed On-Boot-Scan trigger
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   As defense-in-depth against any residual, less predictable timing
   variance that the startup delay alone cannot fully rule out, a radio
   scan that reaches a terminal failure state is automatically re-queued
   and retried a small, bounded number of times (with a short delay
   between attempts) before finally giving up and reporting the genuine
   failure status. This retry mechanism is scoped exclusively to the
   On-Boot-Scan path; the generic controller-initiated scan path is
   unaffected and already gets a fresh trigger per request.

5. Add an early trigger option and result-cache validity check
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   In certain topologies, the radio targeted by On-Boot-Scan can also be
   the one hosting the agent's own backhaul station interface. Once that
   interface associates, the radio's scan resource may no longer be
   available for an explicit trigger, and any scan request issued after
   that point is doomed to fail regardless of delay or retry count.

   To improve the odds of a successful scan in such topologies, a new
   helper is added that triggers On-Boot-Scan early - right after a
   certification reset, while the radio's scan resource is more likely to
   still be free - instead of waiting for the regular, later-firing
   trigger. A companion cache-validity check (true only if every expected
   channel already has a stored SUCCESS result) is used both to decide
   whether this early trigger is actually needed, and to let the later,
   regular trigger skip itself as a no-op if the early trigger already
   produced a complete, valid result set. This avoids wasting the bounded
   retry budget on a second attempt that is now certain to fail.

Together, these changes make On-Boot-Scan results, and their reported
status to the controller, both more reliable to obtain and more accurate
to report.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
